WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:02.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Right before
COVID, two years ago,

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:05.372 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was a speaker for
YAF, not the President.

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:07.107
I was speaking at a
number of campuses,

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.075
one in which was Stanford.

00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:12.879
Three of the students who
invited me to speak there,

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.114
I spoke with beforehand.

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:15.882
I asked them all
sorts of questions,

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:17.684 align:start size:94% position:6%
but one of 'em was how
you became a conservative.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:18.785
Two of the three, very normal.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:20.186
But the third gentleman said,

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:21.755 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Oh no, my parents
are Democrats."

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:23.623
I said, "Well, how'd you
become a conservative?"

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.125
Well, he happened to
be in his dad's truck

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.460
a number of times by
himself driving places

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.928
and would listen on the radio.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he found some
conservative talk radio folks

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:32.832
that he liked listening to.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:34.367
He read what they talked about.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:35.802
He read books.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:38.104 align:start position:0%
By the time he went to Stanford,
his mother's alma mater,

00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:39.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was a full-spectrum
conservative.

00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.775
He was a believer,
he got into this.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:42.976
That's a great story,

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:45.478 align:start size:88% position:13%
but what makes it
powerfully optimistic is

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:48.181 align:start size:84% position:16%
John's mother, John
Rice-Cameron's mother,

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:50.683 align:start size:84% position:16%
happens to be none
other than Susan Rice.

00:00:50.683 --> 00:00:52.652
I look at that and
say, if Susan Rice,

00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:55.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
if only given the opportunity
to hear the truth,

00:00:55.055 --> 00:00:56.523
to hear the facts,

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.591 align:start size:97% position:3%
can actually become a
full-spectrum conservative,

00:00:58.591 --> 00:01:00.760
there's hope for every
young person in America,

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we can just get to
them, and I think we can.

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.965
(upbeat music)

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.805 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I'm Dave Rubin,
and joining me today

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:15.275
is the 45th Governor of the
great State of Wisconsin,

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:19.012 align:start size:97% position:3%
and currently the President
of Young America's Foundation,

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.213
Scott Walker.

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:21.714 align:start size:84% position:16%
Welcome back to
"The Rubin Report".

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:23.450
- Great to be back.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.551
Thanks for having me on.

00:01:24.551 --> 00:01:26.019
- I'm glad to talk to you today,

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:29.155 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we are
only a few days out

00:01:29.155 --> 00:01:33.860 align:start size:88% position:13%
from these very strange
midterm elections.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:34.961
Strange.

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.229 align:start size:88% position:13%
Do you like my
adjective choice there,

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:37.764
or do you have a better one?

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:38.698
- I do.

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:39.632
No, I'm with you on strange.

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.600
I think you're right.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.635
It's not bad, it's not good.

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:45.271
It's just strange, is
a great way to say it.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:46.773
- What's your takeaway?

00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.075 align:start position:0%
I mean, you know, everyone said
this red wave was a-coming,

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:51.044
I kinda was saying it myself,

00:01:51.044 --> 00:01:52.512
but I kept saying, you know,

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.980 align:start size:97% position:3%
"You can't count your
chickens before they hatch,"

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.281
and all that stuff.

00:01:55.281 --> 00:01:56.950 align:start size:88% position:13%
And obviously, we still
have a couple races

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.253
that haven't been announced,
as of us taping this right now.

00:02:00.253 --> 00:02:01.855
But what's your main takeaway?

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:04.691
- Well, a couple
different things.

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:06.593
It's not as big of a
win for Republicans

00:02:06.593 --> 00:02:07.760
as many people thought.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.429 align:start size:84% position:16%
But taking the
House, at a minimum,

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:11.998 align:start position:0%
if that's where we end up
being, I think is a good thing.

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:15.068
'Cause it obviously slows
the horrendous, horrific,

00:02:15.068 --> 00:02:19.038
and almost existential threat
that we have out of Washington

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:21.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
with the Biden administration
and his lackeys there.

00:02:21.141 --> 00:02:25.512 align:start size:97% position:3%
So if for nothing else,
there's at least enough votes

00:02:25.512 --> 00:02:28.281
to make someone other than
Nancy Pelosi the speaker.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:30.416
That's a good thing,
to counter things.

00:02:30.416 --> 00:02:32.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think one of the
big takeaways, though,

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:33.620
I took out of Tuesday,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:34.821
was

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:36.623
that ideas matter,

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:38.191
and that it's not enough

00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.560
just to say you're
against Joe Biden.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:43.163
The polls overwhelmingly
showed dissatisfaction

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.098
with the way Joe Biden's acting,

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:48.067
the way they're handling the
economy, or not handling it.

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.335
So I think unfortunately,

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.037
a lot of candidates
on the right thought,

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:53.373 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Well, we'll talk
about how bad Biden is,

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.074
and we'll just run
as not being Biden."

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.477
And I think voters wanted more.

00:02:57.477 --> 00:02:59.479
And to prove that case,

00:02:59.479 --> 00:03:01.381 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would tell you that
where there were big wins

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:04.584 align:start size:97% position:3%
is every incumbent Republican
governor in the country

00:03:04.584 --> 00:03:07.453 align:start size:94% position:6%
not only won in their
reelections, they won big,

00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:10.657 align:start size:97% position:3%
and in many cases saved some
of the Senate candidates.

00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:12.592
Ohio's a prime example.

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.928
J.D. Vance may not
have won that race

00:03:14.928 --> 00:03:17.864
if it hadn't been for Mike
DeWine winning like big time.

00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
Herschel Walker might
not have been in a runoff

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:22.001
in this Georgia Senate race,

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.970 align:start size:81% position:19%
had Brian Kemp not
won going away.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:25.672
And you know, other governors,

00:03:25.672 --> 00:03:27.240
Sununu in a battleground state,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.075
where unfortunately we didn't
get as close as we thought,

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:30.777
New Hampshire, won big there.

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:32.212
Kim Reynolds in Iowa,

00:03:32.212 --> 00:03:33.713
and obviously the two big ones,

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:37.016
Ron DeSantis in Florida,
and Greg Abbott in Texas.

00:03:37.016 --> 00:03:39.152 align:start size:81% position:19%
I mean, the big
winners on Tuesday

00:03:39.152 --> 00:03:41.221 align:start size:88% position:13%
were Republican
governors, in large part

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:43.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause they didn't
just talk about Biden,

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.392
they actually talked about how
to lead and they acted on it.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:49.596
- Were you surprised that
there wasn't more anger

00:03:49.596 --> 00:03:54.601 align:start position:0%
to some of kind of the Democrat
"stars" of the pandemic?

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.736
I mean a Gretchen Whitmer,

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.671 align:start size:84% position:16%
who had some of
the worst lockdowns

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:01.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
and hypocritical moves in
her personal life during it,

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:03.910
or, you know, it's not like
Gavin Newsom was gonna lose,

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.278
but you know, him in California,

00:04:05.278 --> 00:04:06.579
or even Hochul in New York,

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:08.481
who nobody voted for,
first time around,

00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:11.985
because she obviously came
in after Cuomo was taken out.

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:14.220
But that they didn't
pay any price really.

00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
- No, I was surprised
in that regard,

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.193 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think, particularly
in Michigan,

00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:21.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause we all
still kind of hoped

00:04:21.661 --> 00:04:22.795 align:start size:84% position:16%
that that was a
battleground state.

00:04:22.795 --> 00:04:23.763
I think after Tuesday,

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.231
not just in the governor's race,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:28.735
but you look at even
the regent positions

00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:29.802
in the university system.

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.170
I mean, it has sadly become

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.406 align:start size:81% position:19%
a solidly blue
state in Michigan,

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.141
almost the flip side of Ohio,

00:04:35.141 --> 00:04:36.743
which used to be a
battleground state

00:04:36.743 --> 00:04:38.911 align:start size:94% position:6%
and is pretty consistently
Republican now.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.448 align:start size:88% position:13%
Although you look at
other governors, I mean,

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:45.051 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Democratic
governor in Colorado,

00:04:45.051 --> 00:04:47.320
who I disagree with
on other issues,

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.255
was arguably one
of the better ones

00:04:49.255 --> 00:04:50.556
when it came to the lockdown,

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
and he got rewarded
with a huge win there.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:54.460
So yeah, Polis did, for sure.

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:58.331
And I think obviously the big
winners, Florida and Texas,

00:04:58.331 --> 00:04:59.766
are good examples of people who,

00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:01.301
and I would say just
governors in general,

00:05:01.301 --> 00:05:02.935
not just DeSantis and Abbott,

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:04.437
but they were the
most visible ones.

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:07.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
And remember, detractors said,
"Oh, people are gonna die."

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:08.975 align:start size:84% position:16%
They had a guy dressed
up as a Grim Reaper

00:05:08.975 --> 00:05:11.911
on the beaches in Florida,

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:13.346
and Beto said,

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
"Oh, people are gonna be
dead because of Greg Abbott."

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:16.683
No, they got their states open.

00:05:16.683 --> 00:05:18.885
They not only got their
economy back on track,

00:05:18.885 --> 00:05:20.853
their kids, if you
look at the scores,

00:05:20.853 --> 00:05:22.422
are doing arguably much better

00:05:22.422 --> 00:05:24.957
than kids in states
that were shut down.

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:28.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
But sadly, Michigan,
New York, and California

00:05:28.194 --> 00:05:30.330
are probably now and
the foreseeable future

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.498
pretty solid blue states.

00:05:32.498 --> 00:05:34.334
And people there,

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.302 align:start size:94% position:6%
even though the facts were
right in front of 'em,

00:05:36.302 --> 00:05:38.438
apparently, at least
in these elections,

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:40.473
weren't willing to
budge from that.

00:05:40.473 --> 00:05:42.675
- Do you miss any of the game?

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:45.311 align:start position:0%
Obviously, a two-time governor,
you ran for president,

00:05:45.311 --> 00:05:48.915 align:start position:0%
you know, now you're doing your
thing with young Americans.

00:05:48.915 --> 00:05:50.350
But do you miss the fights?

00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.884 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, when it's election
night, are you like,

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.252
"Ah, I should have got back in,"

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:54.954 align:start size:84% position:16%
or, "I should be
doing this or that"?

00:05:54.954 --> 00:05:58.057 align:start position:0%
- (laughs) My wife was actually
all excited about this.

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
She used to hate those nights
when I was a candidate.

00:06:00.026 --> 00:06:01.594
She was all excited
about it Tuesday.

00:06:01.594 --> 00:06:02.528
I'm like, "Who are you?

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.397
What have you done to my wife?"

00:06:04.397 --> 00:06:07.100 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I don't actually, I don't
miss the campaign stuff,

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:09.569
the election night, the nerves.

00:06:09.569 --> 00:06:11.270
Certainly when I saw
some of the TV ads

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:13.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Wisconsin and elsewhere
across the country,

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.640
I don't miss that.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.143
One of the things I always
felt I would miss and I do,

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:18.644
are some of the people.

00:06:18.644 --> 00:06:20.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
although I'm blessed at
Young America's Foundation

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:22.548
to bump into, not only
in my state, Wisconsin,

00:06:22.548 --> 00:06:25.184
but across the country, some
of the same sorts of folks.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:28.287 align:start size:97% position:3%
And obviously being with these
brave, heroic high school,

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:29.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
college, and now
middle school students

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.592
who are trying to stand out
on their campuses and schools

00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:34.694
as conservatives
is very rewarding.

00:06:34.694 --> 00:06:37.363 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think the biggest thing
I found during COVID,

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.731
people said to me,

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:40.967
"Oh, it must be nice
not to be governor."

00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:42.535
And that was my
initial reaction.

00:06:42.535 --> 00:06:46.005 align:start size:97% position:3%
But after a couple months
of fools across the country,

00:06:46.005 --> 00:06:48.608 align:start size:94% position:6%
in my state and elsewhere,
locking things down,

00:06:48.608 --> 00:06:50.176 align:start size:94% position:6%
as you can imagine, I just
looked at it and said,

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:52.612
"We could be doing
so much better."

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:54.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
People always ask me,
"What would I have done?"

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:56.849
I look at not only
DeSantis and Abbott,

00:06:56.849 --> 00:06:58.718
but Kristi Noem is another
example in South Dakota.

00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:01.387
I said, "I would've put
my trust in the people."

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:02.989
Give people information,

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:06.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
let them know what
the circumstances are.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.361
But put enough faith
in the American people

00:07:08.361 --> 00:07:10.096
and the people in each state

00:07:10.096 --> 00:07:12.498
to let them make the right
decisions for themselves,

00:07:12.498 --> 00:07:14.033
their families, their children.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:17.270
Governors, and for
that matter, mayors

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.038
and others who did that,

00:07:19.038 --> 00:07:21.207
their jurisdictions
are doing far better

00:07:21.207 --> 00:07:22.842 align:start size:84% position:16%
than those that
locked things down.

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:24.143
- I don't know if you
happened to see it,

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:25.945 align:start size:88% position:13%
but there was an article
a couple weeks ago

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.781 align:start size:88% position:13%
in "The Atlantic"
about pandemic amnesty.

00:07:28.781 --> 00:07:29.982
Did you happen to catch that,

00:07:29.982 --> 00:07:31.984 align:start size:84% position:16%
or see any of the
firestorm around that?

00:07:33.319 --> 00:07:34.754
- I did, a little bit.

00:07:34.754 --> 00:07:37.757 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't read the article,
but I did see that.

00:07:37.757 --> 00:07:39.025
- So broadly the idea-

00:07:39.025 --> 00:07:41.127 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's interesting,
looking at all the 2020-

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.062
- Yeah, so the idea of amnesty,

00:07:43.062 --> 00:07:45.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
this idea that we should just
sort of forgive everybody

00:07:45.598 --> 00:07:47.033
who did all of these, you know,

00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:49.035
massive government
overreach things,

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:51.904
and that we let bureaucrats
trample on the Constitution

00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:52.839
and all these things.

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:55.775
I suspect from a Yale studio,

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:57.877
you're not really in
the mood for amnesty.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:02.849 align:start size:88% position:13%
- No, and I think it's,
as you and I know,

00:08:02.849 --> 00:08:06.252 align:start position:0%
sometimes, not only in politics,
but in life in general,

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:07.787
when you come to a crisis,

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:09.455
that's when people
reveal themselves

00:08:09.455 --> 00:08:11.023
to be who they truly are.

00:08:11.023 --> 00:08:12.625 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we saw that
during the pandemic.

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:14.694
The people who not only rushed

00:08:14.694 --> 00:08:16.696
to put the government
in control of things,

00:08:16.696 --> 00:08:18.397
to in many ways disregard

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:21.033 align:start size:88% position:13%
the state and federal
constitutions, you know,

00:08:21.033 --> 00:08:23.970
the Constitution doesn't have
a clause for an emergency.

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:25.505
It's there no matter what.

00:08:25.505 --> 00:08:27.507
And there are a lot of people
who trampled all over that,

00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:29.141 align:start size:97% position:3%
thinking, "Oh, it's an
emergency, it's an emergency."

00:08:29.141 --> 00:08:31.844
No, that's exactly why
you have a Constitution

00:08:31.844 --> 00:08:33.613
is so that, so, you know,

00:08:33.613 --> 00:08:36.215 align:start size:97% position:3%
self-proclaimed emergencies
don't take the day.

00:08:36.215 --> 00:08:37.783 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's what you have
in dictatorships

00:08:37.783 --> 00:08:39.819 align:start size:84% position:16%
and oppressive regimes
around the world.

00:08:39.819 --> 00:08:41.387
We don't have that
in the United States.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.822
We should never forget that.

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:45.892 align:start position:0%
We should never allow something
like this to happen again.

00:08:45.892 --> 00:08:49.595 align:start size:97% position:3%
And again, if you look back,
hindsight's always 20/20,

00:08:49.595 --> 00:08:51.898
but I think it's
very instructive

00:08:51.898 --> 00:08:55.468
to see the governors, the
mayors, the other officials

00:08:55.468 --> 00:08:58.704
who again, put their faith
in, taking this seriously,

00:08:58.704 --> 00:09:02.241
I never once thought it
wasn't a serious issue,

00:09:02.241 --> 00:09:05.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
but who put their
faith in the people.

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.679 align:start size:81% position:19%
I have, one of
the big takeaways,

00:09:06.679 --> 00:09:07.813
and I think you have seen this

00:09:07.813 --> 00:09:09.448
in some of the exit polling,

00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:12.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
was, I think post-pandemic,
there is a shift.

00:09:12.618 --> 00:09:16.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's less about
conservative and liberal,

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:17.657
Democrat and Republican,

00:09:17.657 --> 00:09:19.091
as it was post-pandemic

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:22.762
about blue-collar and
white-collar jobs.

00:09:22.762 --> 00:09:24.964 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think a lot of
people, like my brother,

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
who's a manager at a
convenience store chain,

00:09:27.366 --> 00:09:30.970 align:start position:0%
and my sister-in-law, who sells
appliances for Home Depot,

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:33.072
great, good, hardworking people,

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:35.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
they and other blue-collar
workers like them,

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:37.743
I think became very turned off

00:09:37.743 --> 00:09:40.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
towards the heavy hand
of the government.

00:09:40.613 --> 00:09:43.616 align:start size:88% position:13%
Because first it was
the place they worked at

00:09:43.616 --> 00:09:45.351 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then it was
their kids' schools,

00:09:45.351 --> 00:09:47.453 align:start size:88% position:13%
both of which they
were upset were closed,

00:09:47.453 --> 00:09:49.522
versus, sadly, in the
suburbs in particular,

00:09:49.522 --> 00:09:52.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
a lot of white-collar
employees who were like,

00:09:52.091 --> 00:09:54.794
"Eh, I don't have
to commute to work.

00:09:54.794 --> 00:09:56.696
My kids can each hang
out in their bedrooms,

00:09:56.696 --> 00:10:00.032
be on their computers
all day in school.

00:10:00.032 --> 00:10:01.601
We spend more time together.

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:03.202
This isn't so bad."

00:10:03.202 --> 00:10:04.670
And they were the ones

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.339
that kind of bought into
giving these freedoms away.

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:09.942
I think there's gonna
be a long-term shift

00:10:09.942 --> 00:10:12.411
that reflects of more
suburban voters, sadly,

00:10:12.411 --> 00:10:14.013 align:start size:84% position:16%
being open to
government control,

00:10:14.013 --> 00:10:16.515 align:start size:94% position:6%
and more blue-collar
workers and their families

00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:18.718
who are like, "I don't ever
want this to happen again."

00:10:18.718 --> 00:10:19.919
- Speaking of shifts,

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:21.354 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't know if you've
seen any of this,

00:10:21.354 --> 00:10:23.222 align:start size:97% position:3%
but a lot of people are
saying that, you know, Gen Z,

00:10:23.222 --> 00:10:25.091
which is now voting
for the first time,

00:10:25.091 --> 00:10:27.593
that they're all coming in,
and in essence, you know,

00:10:27.593 --> 00:10:29.795
they're sort of
default leftists.

00:10:29.795 --> 00:10:32.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
They've been taught
that socialism is cool,

00:10:32.898 --> 00:10:34.133
that big government is good,

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:35.835 align:start size:88% position:13%
you should just be given
stuff and all this.

00:10:35.835 --> 00:10:37.770
It's obviously counter to
the work that you guys do.

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:40.406
And I've spoke at many
Young America's events

00:10:40.406 --> 00:10:42.908 align:start size:97% position:3%
and people are excited
about freedom and all that.

00:10:42.908 --> 00:10:44.710
But it is an uphill
battle, for sure.

00:10:46.212 --> 00:10:47.480
- Oh, it totally is,

00:10:47.480 --> 00:10:49.248
because it's starting
younger and younger.

00:10:49.248 --> 00:10:50.783
I mean, we just took over,

00:10:50.783 --> 00:10:52.785
in addition to the Reagan
Ranch out in California,

00:10:52.785 --> 00:10:54.854 align:start size:97% position:3%
this last year, we took
over the Reagan Boyhood Home

00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:57.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the middle of the
country in Dixon, Illinois,

00:10:57.223 --> 00:10:58.424
where we have conferences,

00:10:58.424 --> 00:11:00.026 align:start size:88% position:13%
but we also
occasionally have tours

00:11:00.026 --> 00:11:01.727
and school groups
that come through.

00:11:01.727 --> 00:11:03.095
Our director recently told me

00:11:03.095 --> 00:11:04.563
that some fifth grade students

00:11:04.563 --> 00:11:07.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were there with a
class trip loved the tour,

00:11:07.233 --> 00:11:09.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
but at one point, our
director was talking about

00:11:09.502 --> 00:11:12.004
how Reagan, his actions,

00:11:12.004 --> 00:11:13.739 align:start size:84% position:16%
as we just recognized
this past week,

00:11:13.739 --> 00:11:15.474
you know, November 9th,

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:19.211
the 33rd anniversary of the
Berlin Wall coming down,

00:11:19.211 --> 00:11:21.914
the fall of communism, he's
talking about communism.

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:25.418 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the kid said, "Well,
what's wrong with communism?"

00:11:25.418 --> 00:11:26.686
And the guy was
like, bewildered.

00:11:26.686 --> 00:11:28.120
And he's like, "You
know, we're taught

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:30.356 align:start size:97% position:3%
that that's a system of
government that helps people."

00:11:30.356 --> 00:11:33.292
Like, no! This is not it at all.

00:11:33.292 --> 00:11:35.127
But it's sadly what
we've seen for years

00:11:35.127 --> 00:11:36.629
on college campuses.

00:11:36.629 --> 00:11:38.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
We actually have a suit
against the administrators

00:11:38.597 --> 00:11:41.934 align:start size:97% position:3%
at Clovis Community College
in Fresno, California,

00:11:41.934 --> 00:11:44.437 align:start size:84% position:16%
where during Freedom
Week last year,

00:11:45.905 --> 00:11:47.940 align:start size:97% position:3%
our chapter there had
posters that were approved,

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:49.475
pre-approved to put up.

00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:52.011 align:start size:94% position:6%
The posters showed a woman
in the old Soviet Union

00:11:52.011 --> 00:11:53.279
standing in a grocery store

00:11:53.279 --> 00:11:54.780
where there was no
food on the shelves,

00:11:54.780 --> 00:11:56.949 align:start size:84% position:16%
which sadly was pretty
frequent back then,

00:11:56.949 --> 00:12:00.086
as it is in places like
Cuba and Venezuela today.

00:12:00.086 --> 00:12:02.488
And it said, "Got
milk? Got bread?

00:12:02.488 --> 00:12:04.323
Got blankets? Got anything?"

00:12:04.323 --> 00:12:05.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it was obviously
making a point

00:12:05.758 --> 00:12:08.694 align:start size:84% position:16%
about communism and
socialism not working.

00:12:08.694 --> 00:12:11.897 align:start position:0%
And the administrators
literally took the posters down

00:12:11.897 --> 00:12:13.666
and claimed the reason for that

00:12:13.666 --> 00:12:15.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
was because it was
offensive to Communists.

00:12:15.735 --> 00:12:17.703 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, hell yeah, it's
offensive to Communists.

00:12:17.703 --> 00:12:19.405
That's because they're wrong!

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:20.973
It's the truth!

00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:22.208
We've got to get the truth out,

00:12:22.208 --> 00:12:23.509
and it's gotta happen sooner,

00:12:23.509 --> 00:12:25.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the indoctrination
is happening

00:12:25.177 --> 00:12:26.645 align:start size:84% position:16%
younger and younger
along the way.

00:12:26.645 --> 00:12:28.047
- Yeah, I mean it's incredible

00:12:28.047 --> 00:12:30.316 align:start position:0%
that that's leaking into
elementary school at this point,

00:12:30.316 --> 00:12:32.118
but I suppose at some level,

00:12:32.118 --> 00:12:34.920
none of this should surprise us.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.823 align:start position:0%
Going back to a bit of the
racehorse politics for a moment,

00:12:37.823 --> 00:12:40.760
you know, the big thing
in Republican politics,

00:12:40.760 --> 00:12:42.828
or in conservative politics,
whatever you want to call it,

00:12:42.828 --> 00:12:45.231 align:start size:84% position:16%
is sort of this
Trump/DeSantis thing

00:12:45.231 --> 00:12:49.535 align:start position:0%
that seems to be bursting forth,
whether we like it or not.

00:12:49.535 --> 00:12:52.304
What's your take, and what
do you think is gonna happen?

00:12:54.073 --> 00:12:55.641 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, I think
it'll be interesting

00:12:55.641 --> 00:12:58.010
just because of Ron DeSantis
just having been reelected,

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.745
he's obviously gonna
focus on being governor

00:12:59.745 --> 00:13:00.980
in the short-term.

00:13:00.980 --> 00:13:03.415
Someday, whether
it's 2024 or 2028,

00:13:03.415 --> 00:13:04.984
at some point I think he'll run

00:13:04.984 --> 00:13:06.986
and be an excellent candidate.

00:13:06.986 --> 00:13:09.588
But that may not be the
worst of all scenarios.

00:13:09.588 --> 00:13:11.056
'Cause I think it's pretty clear

00:13:11.056 --> 00:13:12.958 align:start size:88% position:13%
that President Donald
Trump is gonna announce,

00:13:12.958 --> 00:13:15.027
probably within the next week.

00:13:15.027 --> 00:13:17.229
It'll give him a chance, and
all the rest of us a chance,

00:13:17.229 --> 00:13:18.397
to see what happens.

00:13:19.265 --> 00:13:21.834
If he catches fire again,

00:13:21.834 --> 00:13:23.869
it probably makes people
like DeSantis and others

00:13:23.869 --> 00:13:25.070
think twice about running.

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:27.540
If it's lukewarm
or not good at all,

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe it opens the field,
not just to DeSantis,

00:13:30.009 --> 00:13:32.645
but Abbott, Kristi
Noem, Nikki Haley,

00:13:32.645 --> 00:13:34.880
Mike Pompeo, Mike
Pence, Ted Cruz,

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.682 align:start size:84% position:16%
you name it, the whole
universe out there.

00:13:36.682 --> 00:13:38.417
And I think, you know,

00:13:38.417 --> 00:13:40.019 align:start size:81% position:19%
as much as I don't
talk and tweet

00:13:40.019 --> 00:13:41.620
the way that Donald Trump does,

00:13:41.620 --> 00:13:43.489
I do think he's
earned the right,

00:13:43.489 --> 00:13:46.325
based upon what he did during
those four years as President.

00:13:46.325 --> 00:13:48.194 align:start size:88% position:13%
He has, without a
doubt, earned the right

00:13:48.194 --> 00:13:51.063
to be at least at the top of
the list of consideration.

00:13:51.063 --> 00:13:54.233
Doesn't guarantee that
he'll win the nomination

00:13:54.233 --> 00:13:55.668
or be elected President,

00:13:55.668 --> 00:13:57.336
but I think he's
at least earned it.

00:13:57.336 --> 00:13:59.471
I was suspect early
on, but my goodness,

00:13:59.471 --> 00:14:02.942
I mean, tax cuts,
regulatory reform,

00:14:02.942 --> 00:14:06.345
some of the greatest judicial
nominations I've ever seen.

00:14:06.345 --> 00:14:07.580
Even simple things,

00:14:07.580 --> 00:14:09.782
like doing what nobody
else had the guts to do

00:14:09.782 --> 00:14:12.885 align:start size:97% position:3%
and moving the embassy
from Tel Aviv to Jerusalem.

00:14:12.885 --> 00:14:15.321
I mean, there's a whole
cross-section of things

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:17.189
that most of us
as conservatives,

00:14:17.189 --> 00:14:18.791 align:start size:94% position:6%
even from a Libertarian's
point of view,

00:14:18.791 --> 00:14:20.492
never dreamed were possible,

00:14:20.492 --> 00:14:22.194
that this guy got accomplished.

00:14:22.194 --> 00:14:24.263 align:start size:84% position:16%
Now, does he always
talk or tweet

00:14:24.263 --> 00:14:26.465
or have the same tone
that many of us do?

00:14:26.465 --> 00:14:27.733
Probably not.

00:14:27.733 --> 00:14:29.869
But again, I like
people who do more

00:14:29.869 --> 00:14:31.437
than just people who talk,

00:14:31.437 --> 00:14:33.305 align:start size:84% position:16%
and for that he earns
a lot of credit.

00:14:33.305 --> 00:14:34.506
- You mentioned
the Libertarians-

00:14:34.506 --> 00:14:36.075
- I do think the
flip side of it is,

00:14:36.075 --> 00:14:38.043
yeah, he's got a little bit of
a challenge though, I think,

00:14:38.043 --> 00:14:41.714
because many of the people that
he endorsed in the primaries

00:14:41.714 --> 00:14:44.049
either didn't win,
or like with Vance,

00:14:44.049 --> 00:14:45.851
wouldn't have won
without DeWine.

00:14:45.851 --> 00:14:48.387
And so that'll be part
of the equation as well.

00:14:48.387 --> 00:14:49.889
- Right, and again,
as we're taping this,

00:14:49.889 --> 00:14:51.891
we still don't know what's
happening with Kari Lake

00:14:51.891 --> 00:14:54.460 align:start size:84% position:16%
and with Blake
Masters and Laxalt.

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.595
So some of this could shake out
a little bit better for him,

00:14:56.595 --> 00:14:57.763
but we'll kind of see.

00:14:57.763 --> 00:14:59.064 align:start size:88% position:13%
But since you mentioned
the Libertarians,

00:14:59.064 --> 00:15:00.499
on Twitter at least,

00:15:00.499 --> 00:15:02.668
there's always this war
with the Libertarians.

00:15:02.668 --> 00:15:06.538
You know, basically, do we
remain hardcore Libertarians

00:15:06.538 --> 00:15:08.941 align:start size:88% position:13%
and only vote for
Libertarian candidates?

00:15:08.941 --> 00:15:11.944
Or, obviously, we're a bit
closer to being Republicans

00:15:11.944 --> 00:15:13.145
certainly than Democrats.

00:15:13.145 --> 00:15:14.546
Do we align there?

00:15:14.546 --> 00:15:16.115
Again, these are all the ideas

00:15:16.115 --> 00:15:17.249
that Young America's Foundation

00:15:17.249 --> 00:15:18.450
are talking about all the time.

00:15:18.450 --> 00:15:20.019 align:start size:84% position:16%
What do you think
sort of strategically,

00:15:20.019 --> 00:15:21.887
for the person that's out there

00:15:21.887 --> 00:15:24.089 align:start size:84% position:16%
that just, it's
limited government,

00:15:24.089 --> 00:15:28.127 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's low or no taxes,
states' rights, that's it.

00:15:28.127 --> 00:15:29.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
What do you think
they should be doing?

00:15:31.497 --> 00:15:33.165
- Well, I think the
simple answer is

00:15:33.165 --> 00:15:36.402
don't ever let the perfect be
the enemy of the very good.

00:15:36.402 --> 00:15:38.203 align:start size:84% position:16%
And by that I don't
mean just universally,

00:15:38.203 --> 00:15:40.205 align:start size:88% position:13%
if you're a Libertarian,
vote Republican;

00:15:40.205 --> 00:15:42.241 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I do think
don't limit yourself

00:15:42.241 --> 00:15:44.510
to solely voting Libertarian,

00:15:44.510 --> 00:15:46.145
because while that's
great on principle,

00:15:46.145 --> 00:15:47.313
it doesn't get things done.

00:15:47.313 --> 00:15:49.915 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think Reagan certainly
was a great person

00:15:49.915 --> 00:15:51.550
at bringing different
coalitions together.

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:54.720
I hope during my years in
office, three elections,

00:15:54.720 --> 00:15:57.256
of which the recall was one
of the biggest in the nation,

00:15:57.256 --> 00:16:00.059
we were able to do that and
we reigned in government.

00:16:00.059 --> 00:16:01.760
One of the biggest
frustrations to me,

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.996
I don't know if you remember
this, but a few years ago,

00:16:03.996 --> 00:16:06.565
Matt Bevin, the governor of
the Commonwealth of Kentucky,

00:16:06.565 --> 00:16:08.167
arguably at the time

00:16:08.167 --> 00:16:11.603
the most Libertarian-like
candidate in the country,

00:16:11.603 --> 00:16:14.373 align:start size:84% position:16%
a guy who aggressively
cut red tape,

00:16:14.373 --> 00:16:15.841 align:start size:88% position:13%
who aggressively reigned
in the government,

00:16:15.841 --> 00:16:20.546
who cut taxes, who took on
these big issues in Kentucky.

00:16:20.546 --> 00:16:23.582 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he lost that
election just barely,

00:16:23.582 --> 00:16:26.385 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the margin by which
he lost to Beshear,

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:27.886
the current governor,

00:16:27.886 --> 00:16:30.189
was less than the margin

00:16:30.189 --> 00:16:32.558
of what the Libertarian
candidate for governor

00:16:32.558 --> 00:16:33.759
got in that race.

00:16:33.759 --> 00:16:35.461 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so to me, it's
just a great reminder.

00:16:35.461 --> 00:16:37.563
I get it with some of these,

00:16:37.563 --> 00:16:39.031
what some people
might call RINOs,

00:16:39.031 --> 00:16:41.300
people who like a lot
of government spending,

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:42.868 align:start size:84% position:16%
who are just a
little bit less bad

00:16:42.868 --> 00:16:44.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
than the Democrat
running in the race.

00:16:44.837 --> 00:16:46.839
But in that case, it
was a prime example.

00:16:46.839 --> 00:16:48.907 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a guy that
Libertarians should love.

00:16:48.907 --> 00:16:50.409
This is a guy that Libertarians

00:16:50.409 --> 00:16:53.145 align:start size:88% position:13%
should want to replicate
around the country.

00:16:53.145 --> 00:16:54.980
And yet they got
behind a candidate

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.983
who effectively set
that margin down,

00:16:57.983 --> 00:16:59.518
and now you got a full-scale,

00:16:59.518 --> 00:17:02.054 align:start size:97% position:3%
big spending, big government
Democrat in there.

00:17:02.054 --> 00:17:03.555
To me, it just doesn't
make a lot of sense.

00:17:03.555 --> 00:17:05.257
Don't do it across the board.

00:17:05.257 --> 00:17:06.625
Make those candidates,

00:17:06.625 --> 00:17:09.128 align:start size:97% position:3%
those conservative Republican
candidates, earn it.

00:17:09.128 --> 00:17:11.230
And then hold 'em accountable
when they get into office.

00:17:11.230 --> 00:17:12.498
And I think Libertarians

00:17:12.498 --> 00:17:14.266
can make some real
inroads that way.

00:17:14.266 --> 00:17:17.770 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Let's talk about the
other side of the coin here.

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.738
The progressives have basically

00:17:19.738 --> 00:17:21.607 align:start size:84% position:16%
just taken over
the Democrat Party,

00:17:21.607 --> 00:17:23.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think unfortunately,
because of the results

00:17:23.976 --> 00:17:25.911 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are kind of
wishy-washy this week,

00:17:25.911 --> 00:17:28.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think they'll basically
feel emboldened.

00:17:28.714 --> 00:17:30.849
Do you think there's any
stopping that movement

00:17:30.849 --> 00:17:32.217
within the Democrat Party?

00:17:32.217 --> 00:17:33.652
I would imagine,

00:17:33.652 --> 00:17:36.622 align:start size:97% position:3%
I'm not totally well-versed
in Wisconsin politics,

00:17:36.622 --> 00:17:40.125
but I would imagine you've
probably got some moderate,

00:17:40.125 --> 00:17:44.830
or had some moderate Democrats
in Wisconsin back in the day.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:47.499 align:start size:97% position:3%
But they all seem to be gone
at a national level now.

00:17:49.401 --> 00:17:51.003
- Well, even in the states.

00:17:51.003 --> 00:17:53.105
I mean, when I was governor
and certainly years before,

00:17:53.105 --> 00:17:55.707
a long time ago when
I was briefly in the
state legislature,

00:17:55.707 --> 00:17:57.409 align:start size:88% position:13%
but even a few years
ago when I was governor,

00:17:57.409 --> 00:17:58.811
there were still a few

00:17:58.811 --> 00:18:01.213 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the kind of traditional
blue dog outstate,

00:18:01.213 --> 00:18:03.348
somewhat rural-area Democrats

00:18:03.348 --> 00:18:08.220
who were somewhat moderate to
conservative on some issues,

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.721
but you know, were
aligned with unions

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:10.956
and other things along the way.

00:18:10.956 --> 00:18:12.858
They're gone. They're gone.

00:18:12.858 --> 00:18:14.026
Most of those districts,

00:18:14.026 --> 00:18:15.494
it's one of the unique phenomena

00:18:15.494 --> 00:18:18.097 align:start size:94% position:6%
is while the suburbs moved
away from Republicans

00:18:18.097 --> 00:18:19.364
under President Trump,

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:22.234
more I think based on
tone than on policy,

00:18:22.234 --> 00:18:23.635
the flip side of that is

00:18:23.635 --> 00:18:25.771 align:start size:88% position:13%
in a lot of those
rural blue-collar areas

00:18:25.771 --> 00:18:28.140
around my state and
around the country,

00:18:28.140 --> 00:18:31.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
a lot of those Democrats
who'd held those seats before,

00:18:31.243 --> 00:18:32.744
boy, this wave came in,

00:18:32.744 --> 00:18:36.281
and good solid Republican
candidates won those seats.

00:18:36.281 --> 00:18:38.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
So that shift has certainly
happened in Wisconsin,

00:18:38.183 --> 00:18:40.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think arguably
elsewhere in the country.

00:18:40.085 --> 00:18:43.589
And nationally, yeah, I
mean, think about this.

00:18:43.589 --> 00:18:46.692
Joe Biden, who ran just a few
years ago, 2 1/2 years ago,

00:18:46.692 --> 00:18:48.427
Joe Biden on Super Tuesday

00:18:48.427 --> 00:18:50.863 align:start size:84% position:16%
was basically
positioning himself

00:18:50.863 --> 00:18:53.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
as a more mainstream
alternative to Bernie Sanders.

00:18:53.832 --> 00:18:58.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
He eventually got Mayor
Pete and Senator Klobuchar

00:18:58.070 --> 00:18:59.338
out of his lane,

00:18:59.338 --> 00:19:01.573 align:start size:84% position:16%
the mainstream,
more moderate lane,

00:19:01.573 --> 00:19:03.375
because he knew you had
all these other people,

00:19:03.375 --> 00:19:04.376
you had Bernie

00:19:05.344 --> 00:19:06.712
and Elizabeth Warren

00:19:06.712 --> 00:19:08.747
and others in the
far left-wing lane.

00:19:08.747 --> 00:19:11.216
But the irony was he did
that though in the primary

00:19:11.216 --> 00:19:14.119
and then did the opposite of
what traditionally happens.

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:17.322 align:start position:0%
He then went out in the general
and ran hard to the left

00:19:17.322 --> 00:19:18.857
to get Bernie and AOC's support

00:19:18.857 --> 00:19:21.293 align:start size:88% position:13%
and basically outsourced
his agenda to them.

00:19:21.293 --> 00:19:23.996 align:start size:97% position:3%
Remember Kamala Harris
actually had a voting record

00:19:23.996 --> 00:19:25.197
the year before she was picked

00:19:25.197 --> 00:19:27.266
that was further to the left

00:19:27.266 --> 00:19:30.402
than the the self-proclaimed
Socialist, Bernie Sanders.

00:19:30.402 --> 00:19:31.803
And he hasn't stopped since.

00:19:32.971 --> 00:19:34.473
- What do you make of Biden?

00:19:34.473 --> 00:19:35.641
What's going on there?

00:19:35.641 --> 00:19:39.178 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean, is it all
there, what's happening?

00:19:41.146 --> 00:19:42.481
- No, and it's not just age.

00:19:42.481 --> 00:19:43.782
I mean it's a combination.

00:19:43.782 --> 00:19:45.684
You know, as Ronald
Reagan got older,

00:19:45.684 --> 00:19:47.152
even post-presidency,

00:19:47.152 --> 00:19:49.821 align:start size:97% position:3%
and obviously later past
his presidency, Alzheimer's.

00:19:49.821 --> 00:19:51.056
If you have an inner core,

00:19:51.056 --> 00:19:52.991
you still clung to
certain principles.

00:19:52.991 --> 00:19:54.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
One of my complaints
with Joe Biden

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:55.794
far before this presidency

00:19:55.794 --> 00:19:57.596
was Joe Biden would
do and say anything,

00:19:57.596 --> 00:19:58.764
I mean, quite literally.

00:19:58.764 --> 00:20:01.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
In '87, he got nailed
for plagiarizing

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:05.804 align:start size:97% position:3%
a Labor leader over in
Britain, almost word for word,

00:20:05.804 --> 00:20:08.707
claiming his words were
the words of himself

00:20:08.707 --> 00:20:10.209
and not of that Labor leader.

00:20:10.209 --> 00:20:13.412 align:start size:97% position:3%
This guy has consistently done
or said just about anything

00:20:13.412 --> 00:20:14.913
to hold the office of President.

00:20:14.913 --> 00:20:17.716
He ran in the '87/'88 election.

00:20:17.716 --> 00:20:19.251
He ran again in '08.

00:20:19.251 --> 00:20:20.752
Obviously ran in 2020.

00:20:20.752 --> 00:20:23.121 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think this guy
would gnaw off his arm

00:20:23.121 --> 00:20:25.557
before he gave up
the presidency.

00:20:25.557 --> 00:20:29.194 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I just think that's
all the more reason why he,

00:20:29.194 --> 00:20:30.128
per your last question,

00:20:30.128 --> 00:20:32.231
why he's not gonna back away

00:20:32.231 --> 00:20:34.166 align:start size:81% position:19%
from this radical,
radical agenda.

00:20:34.166 --> 00:20:35.133
He can't do it.

00:20:35.133 --> 00:20:36.602
Because if he backs away,

00:20:36.602 --> 00:20:39.504 align:start size:97% position:3%
he doesn't have enough
finesse to avoid a primary.

00:20:39.504 --> 00:20:41.773 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think looking ahead,
that's probably a good thing,

00:20:41.773 --> 00:20:43.342
if you compare it to 1980,

00:20:43.342 --> 00:20:45.877 align:start size:84% position:16%
when we had similar
circumstances.

00:20:45.877 --> 00:20:47.379
If he did back away,

00:20:47.379 --> 00:20:49.047 align:start size:97% position:3%
he'd have what Jimmy Carter
had with Teddy Kennedy,

00:20:49.047 --> 00:20:51.083 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that is a primary
from the left.

00:20:51.083 --> 00:20:53.919
And if he doesn't,
unlike Bill Clinton,

00:20:53.919 --> 00:20:56.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think he doesn't think
he had the same problem

00:20:56.521 --> 00:20:59.091
Bill Clinton had in
the '94 elections,

00:20:59.091 --> 00:21:03.161
I think he's gonna have a
real problem, come 2024.

00:21:03.161 --> 00:21:04.663
But, who knows?

00:21:04.663 --> 00:21:05.998
A lot of us thought things
would be a little different

00:21:05.998 --> 00:21:08.000
on Tuesday in the
elections as well.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:09.501
- Right, and as of
the last couple days,

00:21:09.501 --> 00:21:12.004 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean it sounds, he
says he's running again.

00:21:12.004 --> 00:21:15.540 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's just beyond imagination,
but okay, so be it.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:19.945
So if you guys, if you were
showing up at a YAF event

00:21:19.945 --> 00:21:23.282 align:start size:88% position:13%
at a college campus
that was heavily lefty,

00:21:23.282 --> 00:21:25.317 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is pretty much
every single one of them,

00:21:25.317 --> 00:21:28.053
what are some of the main ideas

00:21:28.053 --> 00:21:29.521
you would want to get across?

00:21:29.521 --> 00:21:31.923 align:start position:0%
One of the things that I've
been talking about a lot lately

00:21:31.923 --> 00:21:34.893
are the Federalist Papers and
the idea of state's rights,

00:21:34.893 --> 00:21:36.461
and making sure that, you know,

00:21:36.461 --> 00:21:39.031
this experiment can
operate differently,

00:21:39.031 --> 00:21:40.399
so that there are
different places

00:21:40.399 --> 00:21:42.901
where things will be different.

00:21:42.901 --> 00:21:44.436
What are some of the ideas

00:21:44.436 --> 00:21:46.471
that you would want to get
across to these young kids?

00:21:47.673 --> 00:21:48.874
- Well, first off, one,

00:21:48.874 --> 00:21:50.375
I think we need to explain it

00:21:50.375 --> 00:21:52.878 align:start size:97% position:3%
in terms that apply not only
the head, but the heart.

00:21:52.878 --> 00:21:54.513 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think that's one
of the mistakes

00:21:54.513 --> 00:21:56.081
conservatives have
made at all ages,

00:21:56.081 --> 00:21:57.716
that we think and
talk with our head,

00:21:57.716 --> 00:21:59.451 align:start size:88% position:13%
the left thinks and
talks with their heart.

00:21:59.451 --> 00:22:00.686
We need to think with our head,

00:22:00.686 --> 00:22:02.421
but talk in ways that
come from the heart.

00:22:02.421 --> 00:22:04.423 align:start size:84% position:16%
And so some of our
most powerful speakers

00:22:04.423 --> 00:22:06.992 align:start size:84% position:16%
at conferences and
increasing on campuses

00:22:06.992 --> 00:22:10.696
are people who've come from
either not Soviet, excuse me,

00:22:10.696 --> 00:22:14.433 align:start size:88% position:13%
communist or socialist,
Marxist-based
countries in the past,

00:22:14.433 --> 00:22:16.802
because they can match
the logic of the argument

00:22:16.802 --> 00:22:18.904 align:start size:84% position:16%
but tie it into
real-world experience.

00:22:18.904 --> 00:22:21.173 align:start size:84% position:16%
We just had a woman
who came, for example,

00:22:21.173 --> 00:22:24.943
and spoke to us, who came
from North Korea years ago,

00:22:24.943 --> 00:22:26.745 align:start size:97% position:3%
escaped with her mother
through China up to Mongolia.

00:22:26.745 --> 00:22:27.913
- Oh, Yeonmi Park.

00:22:27.913 --> 00:22:29.414
She's a good friend.

00:22:29.414 --> 00:22:30.415
And I've had her on the show.
- She's spectacular, right?

00:22:30.415 --> 00:22:31.650
- She's wonderful.

00:22:31.650 --> 00:22:33.852
- Yeah, yeah, she's
a prime example.

00:22:33.852 --> 00:22:35.721
We're gonna have her
out on campuses for us.

00:22:35.721 --> 00:22:36.955
'Cause I love the connection

00:22:36.955 --> 00:22:38.590 align:start size:81% position:19%
of her telling her
personal story.

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:40.525
It's hard to argue from the left

00:22:40.525 --> 00:22:42.260
against things that
she can vouch for,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:43.662
things that actually happened.

00:22:43.662 --> 00:22:45.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
We have a young man
speaking this coming weekend

00:22:45.797 --> 00:22:47.032
from Venezuela.

00:22:47.032 --> 00:22:48.500 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think we need
to do more of that

00:22:48.500 --> 00:22:50.936
and match the logic
with the emotion,

00:22:50.936 --> 00:22:52.204
tying the two together.

00:22:52.204 --> 00:22:54.339
But I also think we
have to get beyond

00:22:54.339 --> 00:22:56.875 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of the
cookie-cutter approach

00:22:56.875 --> 00:22:58.744
of talking in political terms.

00:22:58.744 --> 00:22:59.711
I think you're right.

00:22:59.711 --> 00:23:01.146
Freedom isn't just, you know,

00:23:01.146 --> 00:23:04.316 align:start size:94% position:6%
the opposite of socialism
isn't capitalism.

00:23:04.316 --> 00:23:06.418 align:start size:88% position:13%
Certainly it's free
market-based capitalism

00:23:06.418 --> 00:23:09.988
is a much better route than
government-driven socialism.

00:23:09.988 --> 00:23:11.390
But the opposite of socialism

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:12.758
is more than just
an economic system,

00:23:12.758 --> 00:23:14.426
it's fundamental freedoms.

00:23:14.426 --> 00:23:15.961
One of the ways I talk about it

00:23:15.961 --> 00:23:18.397
is say, it's the difference
between an Uber and a taxi.

00:23:18.397 --> 00:23:21.533
You know, taxis are highly
regulated, highly restricted.

00:23:21.533 --> 00:23:24.136
There's usually a big fee
or tax assigned to it.

00:23:24.136 --> 00:23:26.671
And they try to box people
out from getting into it.

00:23:26.671 --> 00:23:28.673
That's like those on the
left who want to tell you

00:23:28.673 --> 00:23:30.609
what to do, when to
do, and how to do it.

00:23:30.609 --> 00:23:32.411
They put their faith
in the government.

00:23:32.411 --> 00:23:34.813 align:start size:94% position:6%
Our counter should be
kind of like Uber or Lyft,

00:23:34.813 --> 00:23:36.848 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is, you know,
they don't care

00:23:36.848 --> 00:23:38.683
whether you drive once a
day or 100 times a day.

00:23:38.683 --> 00:23:41.353 align:start size:97% position:3%
As long as you get yourself
and your passengers

00:23:41.353 --> 00:23:43.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
safely from one
point to the other,

00:23:44.489 --> 00:23:46.057
they're successful.

00:23:46.057 --> 00:23:49.861 align:start size:88% position:13%
Same view as a
conservative/Libertarian
point of view.

00:23:49.861 --> 00:23:51.630
As long as you don't hurt

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:53.031 align:start size:84% position:16%
the health and safety
of your neighbor,

00:23:53.031 --> 00:23:54.032
go out and live your life.

00:23:54.032 --> 00:23:55.534
Do your thing.

00:23:55.534 --> 00:23:56.768 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't want to tell you what
to do with your business.

00:23:56.768 --> 00:23:58.837 align:start size:88% position:13%
You go pursue your dream
the way you see fit,

00:23:58.837 --> 00:24:00.605
because we believe in you

00:24:00.605 --> 00:24:02.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
more than they believe
in the government.

00:24:02.107 --> 00:24:05.243 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think the more we get
to that fundamental core story

00:24:05.243 --> 00:24:07.612
of what America's all about,

00:24:07.612 --> 00:24:10.115 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's not just conservatives,
it's an American idea,

00:24:10.115 --> 00:24:12.217 align:start size:94% position:6%
the better off we're gonna
be with young people.

00:24:12.217 --> 00:24:13.418
- Are you guys worried at all

00:24:13.418 --> 00:24:15.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
that just the
entropy of all this

00:24:15.487 --> 00:24:17.189
will always drag things left?

00:24:17.189 --> 00:24:18.957
I mean, even right now,
if you think about it,

00:24:18.957 --> 00:24:20.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, there's a big
movement for school choice

00:24:20.992 --> 00:24:22.227
happening on the right.

00:24:22.227 --> 00:24:23.495
So more and more parents

00:24:23.495 --> 00:24:25.297
will select out of
the main systems.

00:24:25.297 --> 00:24:27.199 align:start size:94% position:6%
Personally, I obviously
think that's a good thing,

00:24:27.199 --> 00:24:29.868 align:start size:97% position:3%
and they'll either
homeschool, or charter school,

00:24:29.868 --> 00:24:31.303
or pods or whatever it might be.

00:24:31.303 --> 00:24:32.604
But at the college level,

00:24:32.604 --> 00:24:34.806
that they're either just
not gonna go to college,

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:37.175 align:start size:84% position:16%
or they will just
figure out other ways

00:24:37.175 --> 00:24:38.877
to go into the workforce,

00:24:38.877 --> 00:24:42.247
thus leaving the colleges, in
essence, to the socialists.

00:24:44.082 --> 00:24:45.283
- Yeah, I think you're
right on both counts.

00:24:45.283 --> 00:24:46.751
I mean I've said the reaction,

00:24:46.751 --> 00:24:49.821 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you're a parent being
upset about what we've seen,

00:24:49.821 --> 00:24:51.556
either taught or not
taught in our schools

00:24:51.556 --> 00:24:53.758 align:start size:84% position:16%
over the last few
years, particularly
during the pandemic,

00:24:53.758 --> 00:24:55.227
is one of three things.

00:24:55.227 --> 00:24:56.895
Get involved, run
for school board,

00:24:56.895 --> 00:24:57.929
take your school board over,

00:24:57.929 --> 00:24:59.231
do something that takes

00:24:59.231 --> 00:25:00.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
your traditional
government-run school

00:25:00.599 --> 00:25:02.734
and puts it on the right track.

00:25:02.734 --> 00:25:03.935
Go to a private school.

00:25:03.935 --> 00:25:05.136 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you're in a state
like Wisconsin,

00:25:05.136 --> 00:25:06.571
where I expanded school choice,

00:25:06.571 --> 00:25:08.773 align:start size:84% position:16%
parental school choice
options statewide,

00:25:08.773 --> 00:25:10.342
send your kid to
a private school

00:25:10.342 --> 00:25:12.611
with a voucher or tax credit
or other things like that.

00:25:12.611 --> 00:25:14.513
Or, if you've got the capacity

00:25:14.513 --> 00:25:16.181
and the ability to, homeschool.

00:25:16.181 --> 00:25:18.750
But you're right, in
those last two options,

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:21.119
and certainly when it comes
to colleges and universities,

00:25:21.119 --> 00:25:24.756
that de facto then leaves
a whole mass of people

00:25:24.756 --> 00:25:26.691 align:start size:84% position:16%
in the government-run
institutions

00:25:26.691 --> 00:25:30.595 align:start size:84% position:16%
that don't have a
counter to all that.

00:25:30.595 --> 00:25:32.063
Which is precisely why

00:25:32.063 --> 00:25:33.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
what we're doing at Young
America's Foundation

00:25:33.598 --> 00:25:34.833
is so important,

00:25:34.833 --> 00:25:36.835
YAF.org if people
are interested,

00:25:36.835 --> 00:25:38.470 align:start size:81% position:19%
and that is people
sometimes say,

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:41.439
"Oh, do you have a YAF
chapter at Hillsdale?"

00:25:41.439 --> 00:25:42.908
We do.

00:25:42.908 --> 00:25:44.142
But I'm like, "I'm not
worried about Hillsdale."

00:25:44.142 --> 00:25:45.544
- I think you're going
to be okay at Hillsdale.

00:25:45.544 --> 00:25:47.045 align:start position:0%
- I'm worried about the
University of Wisconsin-Madison.

00:25:47.045 --> 00:25:48.680 align:start size:84% position:16%
Exactly, I'm worried
about Stanford,

00:25:48.680 --> 00:25:50.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm worried about Harvard,
I'm worried about Yale.

00:25:50.015 --> 00:25:51.550
You know, I'm worried about

00:25:51.550 --> 00:25:54.586 align:start size:97% position:3%
so many public,
government-run institutions.

00:25:54.586 --> 00:25:56.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so that's why our work
is certainly not done,

00:25:56.688 --> 00:25:58.557
even as people send their kids

00:25:58.557 --> 00:26:01.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
to alternatives to the
government-run institutions.

00:26:01.026 --> 00:26:03.194
We've gotta fight
every single day

00:26:03.194 --> 00:26:06.631
on every single one of these
campuses to get the truth out.

00:26:06.631 --> 00:26:08.934
And remember, the
irony is, you know,

00:26:08.934 --> 00:26:10.168
free speech is something

00:26:10.168 --> 00:26:12.270
that's protected by
our Constitution,

00:26:12.270 --> 00:26:13.939
but it should be revered,

00:26:13.939 --> 00:26:16.241 align:start size:84% position:16%
of all places, on
our college campuses.

00:26:16.241 --> 00:26:18.777 align:start position:0%
Yet traditionally, that's where
we're at the most at risk.

00:26:18.777 --> 00:26:20.545
And so we're gonna fight there,

00:26:20.545 --> 00:26:22.480 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're gonna fight on campus
and when we get there,

00:26:22.480 --> 00:26:25.183
one of the best things
is, we just had Shapiro,

00:26:25.183 --> 00:26:27.118
Ben Shapiro up the other
day at one of our campuses.

00:26:27.118 --> 00:26:29.854
He typically gets not only
thousands of kids on campus,

00:26:29.854 --> 00:26:31.790
even on these liberal campuses,

00:26:31.790 --> 00:26:33.291
but then we livestream.

00:26:33.291 --> 00:26:36.027
And we put that stuff up on
our YouTube page, @YAFTV,

00:26:36.027 --> 00:26:37.662
and we literally have
millions of people

00:26:37.662 --> 00:26:39.130
tune into those things.

00:26:39.130 --> 00:26:41.900
And so that's how we get
around this other nonsense,

00:26:41.900 --> 00:26:44.803 align:start size:88% position:13%
is finding social media
ways to get around.

00:26:44.803 --> 00:26:46.504
You know, it's the beauty
of not having to go through

00:26:46.504 --> 00:26:48.139
a limited number
of news sources.

00:26:48.139 --> 00:26:50.275 align:start size:84% position:16%
We can put something
up on @YAFTV

00:26:50.275 --> 00:26:53.712
and other social media sites
and really have an impact.

00:26:53.712 --> 00:26:55.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
That's where we get
right to the students.

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:56.548
Not only in college;

00:26:56.548 --> 00:26:58.316
high school, middle
school, and younger.

00:26:58.316 --> 00:27:00.552
- What would you say
to the high school kid,

00:27:00.552 --> 00:27:02.654
or I guess to the parents
of the high school kid,

00:27:02.654 --> 00:27:04.589
that care about these ideas,

00:27:04.589 --> 00:27:07.392 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they're not ready to not
let the kid go to college

00:27:07.392 --> 00:27:08.760
or just send them out
into the real world?

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.228
They want to send them
to one of these schools,

00:27:10.228 --> 00:27:12.430 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then they're just simply
looking at the numbers

00:27:12.430 --> 00:27:13.365
and they're going,

00:27:13.365 --> 00:27:15.000
"Man, it's now $60 grand a year

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:16.668
to go to, I don't
know, Syracuse,

00:27:16.668 --> 00:27:19.104
and it's $20 grand a year
to go to a state school.

00:27:19.104 --> 00:27:20.905
Like, this just does not work."

00:27:20.905 --> 00:27:22.507
What do you say to those people?

00:27:23.808 --> 00:27:25.010
- Well, I'd say, one,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:27.345 align:start size:88% position:13%
increasingly, there
are better alternatives

00:27:27.345 --> 00:27:29.981
than sending a young person
to a four-year undergraduate.

00:27:29.981 --> 00:27:32.517
Certainly if you want to be an
engineer, a doctor, a lawyer,

00:27:32.517 --> 00:27:34.252 align:start size:97% position:3%
those are certain professions
where you have to.

00:27:34.252 --> 00:27:35.687
But I'd think twice.

00:27:35.687 --> 00:27:36.955
There's a lot of,

00:27:36.955 --> 00:27:38.590
and I wish I wasn't
having to say that,

00:27:38.590 --> 00:27:40.592
but I think between the cost,

00:27:40.592 --> 00:27:42.694
the rising cost, which is
only going to get worse.

00:27:42.694 --> 00:27:45.497 align:start size:84% position:16%
The so-called student
debt reduction

00:27:45.497 --> 00:27:47.198
is only going to open the door

00:27:47.198 --> 00:27:49.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
towards more of these
colleges and universities

00:27:49.034 --> 00:27:51.236
upping their tuition
by $10 grand or more.

00:27:51.236 --> 00:27:52.871 align:start size:81% position:19%
So it gets offset,
in that regard.

00:27:52.871 --> 00:27:55.607
But I also think just the
radical indoctrination.

00:27:55.607 --> 00:27:56.841
If you're going to go, though,

00:27:56.841 --> 00:27:58.510
if your kid, your
son or daughter,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:01.212
grandson or granddaughter, or
you as a student are gonna go,

00:28:01.212 --> 00:28:02.447
one of the most
important things,

00:28:02.447 --> 00:28:03.982 align:start size:84% position:16%
whether it's YAF,
College Republicans,

00:28:03.982 --> 00:28:05.483
some other conservative group,

00:28:05.483 --> 00:28:08.720
is surround yourself with
as many like-minded people.

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:10.922 align:start size:94% position:6%
'Cause you're gonna get
exposed to different ideas

00:28:10.922 --> 00:28:12.390
over and over again.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.526 align:start size:94% position:6%
But you want to have a
support network out there.

00:28:14.526 --> 00:28:16.361
The number one thing we
hear at our conferences,

00:28:16.361 --> 00:28:17.629
our students say,

00:28:17.629 --> 00:28:19.164
"Oh, it's so nice
to be around kids

00:28:19.164 --> 00:28:20.699
that think the same way we do."

00:28:20.699 --> 00:28:23.968
And I know with both of my
sons, who are 28 and 27 now,

00:28:23.968 --> 00:28:26.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
one who went to a private
Jesuit institution,

00:28:26.905 --> 00:28:28.573
which sadly was woke as well,

00:28:28.573 --> 00:28:30.542 align:start position:0%
the other went to the
University of Wisconsin-Madison,

00:28:30.542 --> 00:28:32.644
which is left of Berkeley.

00:28:32.644 --> 00:28:34.479
Both of 'em came
out conservatives

00:28:34.479 --> 00:28:36.981
because of the people they
surrounded themselves with,

00:28:36.981 --> 00:28:39.417
who to this day still
are allies of theirs.

00:28:39.417 --> 00:28:40.919 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think that's
incredibly important.

00:28:40.919 --> 00:28:42.287 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, what do
you say to the kids

00:28:42.287 --> 00:28:43.755
once they get through it,

00:28:43.755 --> 00:28:45.724
when then they look at
the economy, and they go,

00:28:45.724 --> 00:28:48.860
"Man, these jobs that we're
being offered, we basically,

00:28:48.860 --> 00:28:51.129 align:start size:97% position:3%
we can't live in big
cities 'cause rent is high,

00:28:51.129 --> 00:28:53.798
and we do want to get out
of our parents' basement,

00:28:53.798 --> 00:28:56.267 align:start size:94% position:6%
but seems like everybody's
going back in there."

00:28:58.470 --> 00:28:59.771
- Well, again, that's
part of the reason why

00:28:59.771 --> 00:29:01.740
I say reluctantly, but sadly,

00:29:01.740 --> 00:29:04.876
that if you don't have
to be in a profession

00:29:04.876 --> 00:29:07.145 align:start size:97% position:3%
where it requires a four-year
undergraduate degree,

00:29:07.145 --> 00:29:08.680
I'd say find some
other alternatives.

00:29:08.680 --> 00:29:11.316
A lot of folks, community
colleges, apprenticeships,

00:29:11.316 --> 00:29:13.685 align:start size:88% position:13%
other means to get
into decent paying jobs

00:29:13.685 --> 00:29:15.854
without that student loan debt.

00:29:15.854 --> 00:29:17.722
On top of it, it's a
good reminder again,

00:29:17.722 --> 00:29:20.191 align:start size:81% position:19%
why socialism,
communism, Marxism

00:29:20.191 --> 00:29:22.093
might sound good to
some on the surface,

00:29:22.093 --> 00:29:23.261
but it just doesn't work.

00:29:23.261 --> 00:29:24.796
Not only in past history,

00:29:24.796 --> 00:29:26.364 align:start size:88% position:13%
it doesn't work in
Venezuela or Cuba today.

00:29:26.364 --> 00:29:28.566 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's certainly a
problem in Hong Kong.

00:29:28.566 --> 00:29:30.535
We've seen it not that long
ago in the Soviet Union.

00:29:30.535 --> 00:29:32.370
But even in big cities,

00:29:32.370 --> 00:29:34.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
you see so many big
cities with the problems,

00:29:34.672 --> 00:29:36.074
not only with high prices

00:29:36.074 --> 00:29:38.376 align:start size:84% position:16%
and out-of-control
rent or housing costs.

00:29:38.376 --> 00:29:40.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
You see it with
cost-of-living problems.

00:29:40.111 --> 00:29:42.914
You see it with crime in
a lot of our large cities.

00:29:42.914 --> 00:29:44.382
Those are all places

00:29:44.382 --> 00:29:46.684 align:start size:97% position:3%
where you not just have
Democrat or liberal control,

00:29:46.684 --> 00:29:50.789
you largely have radicals in
charge of these governments.

00:29:50.789 --> 00:29:53.191
And I think that's got to
be the constant pushback,

00:29:53.191 --> 00:29:54.726
the wake-up call.

00:29:54.726 --> 00:29:56.494 align:start size:88% position:13%
If you're concerned
about all these things,

00:29:56.494 --> 00:29:59.764
stop voting for people
who embrace these ideas,

00:29:59.764 --> 00:30:01.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you're gonna
get more of it.

00:30:01.199 --> 00:30:02.467
- What do you think we do

00:30:02.467 --> 00:30:03.902
about the tension
between, I think,

00:30:03.902 --> 00:30:06.037 align:start position:0%
what's growing between the
urban and suburban communities?

00:30:06.037 --> 00:30:08.640 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, Lee Zeldin obviously
did an incredible job

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:10.341
in New York, way better
than anyone thought.

00:30:10.341 --> 00:30:11.576
But he did lose.

00:30:11.576 --> 00:30:13.144 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I looked at
the map, you know,

00:30:13.144 --> 00:30:14.612
everybody was showing
the map of New York.

00:30:14.612 --> 00:30:17.282 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if you look at the
map of New York, it's red.

00:30:17.282 --> 00:30:18.550
It's almost all red.

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.485
Then there's the New
York City metro area

00:30:20.485 --> 00:30:22.287
and a little bit sort
of towards Buffalo.

00:30:22.287 --> 00:30:24.088
That's pretty much it.

00:30:24.088 --> 00:30:26.825
But yet, they all have
to vote for one governor.

00:30:26.825 --> 00:30:30.628
And I think that tension
is almost intractable.

00:30:30.628 --> 00:30:32.096
And it's not,

00:30:32.096 --> 00:30:33.331 align:start size:97% position:3%
New York's not the only
place with that, obviously.

00:30:34.299 --> 00:30:36.367
- No, you see it in variations.

00:30:36.367 --> 00:30:40.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
You see it, even in the New
York metro area, the citywide.

00:30:40.438 --> 00:30:41.573
You look at the difference,

00:30:41.573 --> 00:30:43.508 align:start size:88% position:13%
the last three
gubernatorial elections,

00:30:43.508 --> 00:30:45.743
I was just looking
at an interesting
map today about that,

00:30:45.743 --> 00:30:48.079
where Manhattan,
the island there,

00:30:48.079 --> 00:30:50.682
has gotten deeper
and deeper blue.

00:30:50.682 --> 00:30:52.617
You see Staten Island's
gotten more and more red.

00:30:52.617 --> 00:30:54.819
And then you see pockets based,

00:30:54.819 --> 00:30:58.223
interestingly enough,
based on ethnic trends.

00:30:58.223 --> 00:31:00.592 align:start size:84% position:16%
You see a lot of
folks fleeing Russia

00:31:00.592 --> 00:31:02.694
that have come into
southern Brooklyn

00:31:02.694 --> 00:31:03.928
that actually have made it

00:31:03.928 --> 00:31:05.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
a little bit more
open to Republicans.

00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:08.399 align:start size:88% position:13%
You see in the
northeast part of Queens

00:31:08.399 --> 00:31:10.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
where you see,
according to this map,

00:31:10.201 --> 00:31:13.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was some of the increases
in Asian population,

00:31:13.037 --> 00:31:14.939
and that's made it a
little bit more open.

00:31:14.939 --> 00:31:17.709 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you see these
divisions out there,

00:31:17.709 --> 00:31:18.977
not only just political,

00:31:18.977 --> 00:31:21.512
but by groups of people
coming into the country

00:31:21.512 --> 00:31:22.914
or coming into a given region.

00:31:22.914 --> 00:31:24.082
We see it though, nationwide.

00:31:24.082 --> 00:31:25.783
In my state, in Wisconsin,

00:31:25.783 --> 00:31:27.919 align:start size:84% position:16%
part of the reason why
the governor's race

00:31:27.919 --> 00:31:29.153
was not within reach,

00:31:29.153 --> 00:31:31.222
other than the fact that
the Democrat incumbent

00:31:31.222 --> 00:31:33.558
spent about twice as much
money as the Republican,

00:31:33.558 --> 00:31:36.094
but was Madison, where
the state capital is at

00:31:36.094 --> 00:31:37.562
and where the university is at,

00:31:37.562 --> 00:31:41.299 align:start size:97% position:3%
got much higher percentages,
not only of total votes,

00:31:41.299 --> 00:31:44.469 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a much higher
margin for Democrats.

00:31:44.469 --> 00:31:46.137
The suburbs were
still Republican,

00:31:46.137 --> 00:31:48.840
but not as much as they
had been in the past.

00:31:48.840 --> 00:31:51.976
And the outstate was a little
bit more open to Republicans.

00:31:51.976 --> 00:31:53.444
But you see this split

00:31:53.444 --> 00:31:56.614 align:start size:97% position:3%
between urban areas becoming
increasingly liberal;

00:31:57.715 --> 00:31:59.284
not even just suburban,

00:31:59.284 --> 00:32:03.488
but more rural to mid-sized
communities becoming strong,

00:32:03.488 --> 00:32:06.357 align:start size:97% position:3%
steady strongholds, I
should say, for Republicans.

00:32:06.357 --> 00:32:07.792
There's just a real
division in America

00:32:07.792 --> 00:32:09.227
and we've got to get past that.

00:32:09.227 --> 00:32:12.130 align:start position:0%
I would hope, one of the things
we talk to students about,

00:32:12.130 --> 00:32:15.600
is countering what the left
does over and over again,

00:32:15.600 --> 00:32:17.769
certainly on campus,
but even in society.

00:32:17.769 --> 00:32:21.306 align:start position:0%
If they disagree with you, they
call you a series of names.

00:32:21.306 --> 00:32:23.341
You're racist, you're sexist,

00:32:23.341 --> 00:32:24.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're transphobic,
whatever it is.

00:32:24.575 --> 00:32:25.810
Sometimes it doesn't even matter

00:32:25.810 --> 00:32:27.812
if that's the topic
you're talking about.

00:32:27.812 --> 00:32:29.714
That's their way of
intimidating people

00:32:29.714 --> 00:32:31.916
out of real debate,
real discussion.

00:32:31.916 --> 00:32:34.786 align:start size:84% position:16%
Our counter, for those
of us center-right,

00:32:34.786 --> 00:32:36.621
can't be coming up with
our own series of names,

00:32:36.621 --> 00:32:38.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think rather pushing
this larger narrative

00:32:38.656 --> 00:32:40.358 align:start size:84% position:16%
that really is
about unity, saying,

00:32:40.358 --> 00:32:43.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Hey, I don't care whether
you're Black or white,

00:32:43.528 --> 00:32:44.829
young or old, rich or poor.

00:32:44.829 --> 00:32:46.197
I don't care whether
you were born here

00:32:46.197 --> 00:32:47.632 align:start size:88% position:13%
or you legally came here
from somewhere else.

00:32:47.632 --> 00:32:50.001 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't care about your
orientation or anything else.

00:32:50.001 --> 00:32:51.235
What I care about

00:32:51.235 --> 00:32:53.538 align:start size:84% position:16%
is that you should
have the same freedoms

00:32:53.538 --> 00:32:55.039
and the same opportunities

00:32:55.039 --> 00:32:57.342
as I inherited and
others have inherited

00:32:57.342 --> 00:32:58.876
from our parents
and grandparents."

00:32:58.876 --> 00:33:00.111
That's the American Dream.

00:33:00.111 --> 00:33:01.379
That's a bigger issue

00:33:01.379 --> 00:33:03.614
than just conservative
or liberal out there.

00:33:03.614 --> 00:33:06.050 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's one that truly
does bring people together.

00:33:06.050 --> 00:33:08.953
Because then we can start
to see, yeah, you know,

00:33:08.953 --> 00:33:10.788 align:start size:84% position:16%
the why is what
matters, not the what.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:12.190
The what will cause us

00:33:12.190 --> 00:33:14.359
to have political and
other differences.

00:33:14.359 --> 00:33:15.994
But if we believe in the why,

00:33:15.994 --> 00:33:18.896
the idea that that all
of us were created equal,

00:33:18.896 --> 00:33:20.698 align:start size:94% position:6%
that the government didn't
give us these rights,

00:33:20.698 --> 00:33:22.433
that God gave us these rights,

00:33:22.433 --> 00:33:24.669
and it's the role of the
government fundamentally

00:33:24.669 --> 00:33:26.070
to protect those rights,

00:33:26.070 --> 00:33:27.538
not the least of which

00:33:27.538 --> 00:33:28.806
are life, liberty, and
the pursuit of happiness.

00:33:28.806 --> 00:33:30.641
If that we agree on,

00:33:30.641 --> 00:33:33.211
then we can debate the
specifics of the what.

00:33:33.211 --> 00:33:36.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
But right now, sadly, in
most places in this country,

00:33:36.114 --> 00:33:38.282
people don't even agree on
what they think the why is.

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:39.851
And that's a big problem.

00:33:39.851 --> 00:33:41.252
- Yeah, it's funny,
my audience knows,

00:33:41.252 --> 00:33:43.321 align:start size:84% position:16%
I like to end the
show with some hope,

00:33:43.321 --> 00:33:46.090
and we actually play a lot of
Ronald Reagan clips at the end

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:48.393
to give people some sort
of aspirational message.

00:33:48.393 --> 00:33:51.629
I certainly know that type
of thing will work for you.

00:33:51.629 --> 00:33:52.797
But are you,

00:33:52.797 --> 00:33:53.731
you sort of just answered this,

00:33:53.731 --> 00:33:55.233
but are you hopeful

00:33:55.233 --> 00:33:58.536
that if we can teach enough
young people that stuff,

00:33:58.536 --> 00:34:00.438
that we will reverse this?

00:34:00.438 --> 00:34:02.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because I think
what's happening is

00:34:02.073 --> 00:34:03.641
a lot of people now feel,

00:34:03.641 --> 00:34:06.310
if you believe in freedom,
you're hopeful in red states,

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:09.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the blue state people
are giving up hope.

00:34:09.280 --> 00:34:11.549
And maybe they'll just end
up moving to the red states,

00:34:11.549 --> 00:34:12.850
and it is what it is.

00:34:12.850 --> 00:34:15.686 align:start size:88% position:13%
But the hope in terms
of a national structure,

00:34:15.686 --> 00:34:16.721
I think is eroding.

00:34:18.423 --> 00:34:22.193
- I'm 100% hopeful, and I'll
tell you one quick story why.

00:34:22.193 --> 00:34:23.761 align:start size:84% position:16%
Right before COVID,
two years ago,

00:34:23.761 --> 00:34:27.465 align:start size:88% position:13%
I was a speaker for
YAF, not the President.

00:34:27.465 --> 00:34:29.200
I was speaking at a
number of campuses,

00:34:29.200 --> 00:34:31.202
one of which was Stanford.

00:34:31.202 --> 00:34:34.972
Three of the students who
invited me to speak there,

00:34:34.972 --> 00:34:36.174
I spoke with beforehand.

00:34:36.174 --> 00:34:37.942
I asked them all
sorts of questions,

00:34:37.942 --> 00:34:39.744 align:start size:94% position:6%
but one of 'em was how
you became a conservative.

00:34:39.744 --> 00:34:40.878
Two of the three, very normal.

00:34:40.878 --> 00:34:42.213
But the third gentleman said,

00:34:42.213 --> 00:34:43.815 align:start size:81% position:19%
"Oh no, my parents
are Democrats."

00:34:43.815 --> 00:34:45.383
I said, "Well, how'd you
become a conservative?"

00:34:45.383 --> 00:34:47.185
Well, he happened to
be in his dad's truck

00:34:47.185 --> 00:34:49.520
a number of times by
himself driving places

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:51.055
and would listen on the radio.

00:34:51.055 --> 00:34:53.691 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he found some
conservative talk radio folks

00:34:53.691 --> 00:34:54.926
that he liked listening to.

00:34:54.926 --> 00:34:56.427
He read what they talked about.

00:34:56.427 --> 00:34:57.829
He read books.

00:34:57.829 --> 00:35:00.131 align:start position:0%
By the time he went to Stanford,
his mother's alma mater,

00:35:00.131 --> 00:35:01.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
he was a full-spectrum
conservative.

00:35:01.899 --> 00:35:03.768
He was a believer,
he got into this.

00:35:03.768 --> 00:35:04.969
That's a great story.

00:35:04.969 --> 00:35:06.671 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what makes it
powerfully optimistic

00:35:06.671 --> 00:35:10.174
is John's mother, John
Rice-Cameron's mother,

00:35:10.174 --> 00:35:12.643 align:start size:84% position:16%
happens to be none
other than Susan Rice.

00:35:12.643 --> 00:35:14.645
I look at that and
say, if Susan Rice,

00:35:14.645 --> 00:35:17.048 align:start size:97% position:3%
if only given the opportunity
to hear the truth,

00:35:17.048 --> 00:35:18.483
to hear the facts,

00:35:18.483 --> 00:35:20.585 align:start size:97% position:3%
can actually become a
full-spectrum conservative,

00:35:20.585 --> 00:35:22.787
there's hope for every
young person in America,

00:35:22.787 --> 00:35:25.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
if we can just get to
them, and I think we can.

00:35:25.389 --> 00:35:26.824
- So the question really is,

00:35:26.824 --> 00:35:29.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
is Susan Rice's
son working on her?

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:32.063 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's really the the question
that we must be asking.

00:35:33.598 --> 00:35:34.499
Whether we'll find out.

00:35:34.499 --> 00:35:36.100
- Well, he's a good guy.

00:35:36.100 --> 00:35:37.602
Exactly.

00:35:37.602 --> 00:35:38.836
He loves his parents, he
just thinks they're wrong.

00:35:38.836 --> 00:35:41.939
And thank God he got
exposed to the truth.

00:35:41.939 --> 00:35:43.474
- Governor Scott Walker,

00:35:43.474 --> 00:35:44.642
I appreciate the time and
all the work you're doing.

00:35:44.642 --> 00:35:45.643
Thank you very much.

00:35:46.911 --> 00:35:48.045
- Thank you.

